@danieldegrasse  Could you please clarify the specific issue we're trying to address here and provide steps to reproduce it?

In the terminals I've used, such as picocom and PuTTY, when a command exceeds the terminal's width, the terminal automatically wraps the cursor to the next line while printing the command. I haven't encountered a scenario where it was necessary to manually manage the end of the line and reposition the cursor during command reprinting. Based on my experience, tracking the cursor position is only relevant when navigating the command using arrow keys.

The only related issue I'm aware of, which I decided not to address due to its low priority, is handling wrapping when a command is longer than the entire terminal window—specifically, when it spans more lines than the terminal can display.
